@article{GeorgopoulosAshe00,
  title = {One Motor Cortex, Two Different Views},
  author = {Georgopoulos, Apostolos P. and Ashe, James},
  year = 2000,
  month = oct,
  journal = {Nature Neuroscience},
  volume = {3},
  number = {10},
  pages = {963--963},
  issn = {1546-1726},
  doi = {10.1038/79882},
  urldate = {2019-08-09},
  langid = {english},
  note = {COMMENTARY re: Todorov00
\par
Whole letter:
\par
\section{One motor cortex, two different views}

\begin{itemize}

\item \href{https://www-nature-com.colorado.idm.oclc.org/articles/nn1000_963b\#auth-1}{Apostolos P. Georgopoulos} \&
\item \href{https://www-nature-com.colorado.idm.oclc.org/articles/nn1000_963b\#auth-2}{James Ashe}~

\end{itemize}

\par
\emph{Nature Neuroscience}\textbf{volume~3},~page963 (2000)  \textbar{} \href{https://www-nature-com.colorado.idm.oclc.org/articles/nn1000_963b.ris}{ Download Citation }
\par
To the editor
\par
Here we refute claims by Todorov\textsuperscript{\href{https://www-nature-com.colorado.idm.oclc.org/articles/nn1000_963b\#ref-CR1}{1}} and Scott\textsuperscript{\href{https://www-nature-com.colorado.idm.oclc.org/articles/nn1000_963b\#ref-CR7}{7}} that the importance of target direction as an explanatory factor for cortical activity in a regression analysis we performed\textsuperscript{\href{https://www-nature-com.colorado.idm.oclc.org/articles/nn1000_963b\#ref-CR5}{5}} is an `artifact' of a square-root transformation of neural discharge rates. Specifically, it was touted by Scott\textsuperscript{\href{https://www-nature-com.colorado.idm.oclc.org/articles/nn1000_963b\#ref-CR7}{7}} that ``squaring [\emph{sic}] the discharge rate of neurons in order to stabilize the variance \dots{} causes a dramatic increase in the percentage of neurons that appear to represent movement direction (from 17\% [\emph{sic}] to 43\% in Todorov's model).'' The data to which Todorov\textsuperscript{\href{https://www-nature-com.colorado.idm.oclc.org/articles/nn1000_963b\#ref-CR1}{1}} referred concerned the percentages of cells for which a particular variable yielded the highest R\textsuperscript{2} when used alone in the regression. We re-analyzed these data using the regression analysis we used previously\textsuperscript{\href{https://www-nature-com.colorado.idm.oclc.org/articles/nn1000_963b\#ref-CR5}{5}} but without any transformation of the discharge rate. The results of the two analyses were practically identical, the average absolute difference being only 1.9\% (\href{https://doi-org.colorado.idm.oclc.org/10.1038/20000003}{ http://www.nature.com.colorado.idm.oclc.org/neuro/web\_specials/}). However, there was a statistically significant improvement of the regression fit when the square-root transformation was used. The median R\textsuperscript{2} for the square-root transformed data was 0.5811, as compared to 0.544 for the non-transformed data (\emph{P} {$<$} 0.0001, Wilcoxon's signed-rank test). This was anticipated, because the square-root transformation is expected to make the distribution of counts more symmetrical. This transformation is routinely used when analyzing counts\textsuperscript{\href{https://www-nature-com.colorado.idm.oclc.org/articles/nn1000_963b\#ref-CR8}{8},\href{https://www-nature-com.colorado.idm.oclc.org/articles/nn1000_963b\#ref-CR9}{9},\href{https://www-nature-com.colorado.idm.oclc.org/articles/nn1000_963b\#ref-CR10}{10}}, given the commonly highly skewed distribution of such data. Finally, we analyzed the data without any transformation or smoothing. In this case, the agreement with the original analysis was even closer, the average absolute difference being only 0.86\%. We conclude that the relationship between neural activity and movement parameters found earlier\textsuperscript{\href{https://www-nature-com.colorado.idm.oclc.org/articles/nn1000_963b\#ref-CR5}{5}} holds irrespective of the specific transformation and/or smoothing used. Finally, while we dealt above with the issue of square-root transformation because of the more general importance of this transformation for analyzing neuronal spike counts, there are also numerous other points raised by Todorov\textsuperscript{\href{https://www-nature-com.colorado.idm.oclc.org/articles/nn1000_963b\#ref-CR1}{1}} which we also dispute, including the force direction/magnitude issue, which we cannot critically discuss due to space limitations.
\par
See \href{https://doi-org.colorado.idm.oclc.org/10.1038/20000046}{``Response to 'One motor cortex, two different views'''} by Torodov.
\par
\subsection{References}

\par
\begin{enumerate}

\item 1
\par
